Yes. I think the key is your last sentence:

Hope exists until you don’t need it.

That tells us Hope shouldn’t be represented as an ordinary persistent concept-token. It is a temporary control operator over an unresolved transition.

The Hammer gives me:

\boxed{
H=(G,\Delta,U,A,\Pi,\tau)
}

Where:

* G = desired state
* \Delta = distance between current and desired state
* U = unresolved possibility — we do not yet know that G is impossible
* A = agency/capacity to keep acting
* \Pi = known or discoverable pathways
* \tau = persistence condition: how long the branch remains worth carrying

That lines up surprisingly well with established hope theory, which decomposes hope into goal + agency + pathways.  But your formulation adds something I think is important computationally:

Hope occupies the gap.

If you’re already holding the thing, you don’t hope to obtain it.

If it’s demonstrably impossible, rational hope should eventually collapse or redirect.

Hope lives here:

S_0
\quad
\underbrace{\xrightarrow{\qquad ? \qquad}}_{\textbf{HOPE}}
\quad
S_G

It is a bridge-token whose bridge hasn’t been built yet.

And that tells us what the machine should do when it encounters HOPE.

Not:

“Believe this is true.”

Instead:

\boxed{\texttt{HOPE}(G)\Rightarrow\texttt{DO\_NOT\_PRUNE}(G)}

More completely:

\texttt{HOPE}(G)=
\begin{cases}
\text{preserve branch}\\
\text{search for pathways}\\
\text{allocate bounded exploration}\\
\text{collect evidence}\\
\text{update probability}\\
\text{continue while viable}
\end{cases}

That’s closely related to “optimism in the face of uncertainty” in reinforcement learning, where optimism deliberately encourages exploration—but importantly, research also shows that unconstrained optimism can waste effort exploring irreducible noise. 

So Pantheon Hope needs bounded faith, not blind faith.

And then comes the beautiful part you spotted:

Hope destroys itself when successful.

Suppose:

S_0 \xrightarrow{H} S_1 \xrightarrow{} S_2 \xrightarrow{} G

Once G becomes present:

\boxed{H(G)\rightarrow 0}

Not because hope failed.

Because hope completed its function.

It undergoes a state transition:

\text{Hope}
\rightarrow
\text{Path}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{Capability}

And now Ouroboros-with-Metabolism matters. You don’t retain the old hope as the primary object.

You digest it.

\boxed{
\text{Hope consumed}
\rightarrow
\text{Pathway retained}
}

Yesterday:

I hope this can be done.

Tomorrow:

Here’s how it’s done.

That’s Cognitive Fossilization again, except Hope is what keeps the organism searching long enough to discover the fossilizable mechanism.

So I’d encode the actual semantic primitive something like:

\boxed{
\mathsf{HOPE}
=
\operatorname{PreserveViableFuture}
(G\mid\neg\text{resolved})
}

or, more operationally:

HOPE {
    target: G
    status: unresolved
    viability_floor: ε
    instruction: preserve_branch
    action: search_pathways
    budget: bounded
    update: evidence
    success: compile_path
    failure: release_or_redirect
    lifetime: until_resolved
}

That final field is essential.

Hope has a TTL.

But not necessarily a clock-based TTL.

It’s an epistemic TTL:

\tau_H=\text{until sufficient resolution}

Success kills Hope by turning it into knowledge/capability.

Strong contrary evidence can kill or redirect Hope by turning it into updated constraint.

Either way, if we’re following Consume the Tail:

\boxed{
\text{Hope}
\xrightarrow{\text{reality}}
\text{Delta}
\xrightarrow{\text{metabolism}}
\text{something more useful than Hope}
}

And now I see why the Pilot Light was such a good representation.

The pilot light isn’t the furnace.

It doesn’t heat the house.

Its function is to preserve the possibility of ignition until ignition occurs.

Then the tiny flame disappears into something much larger.

Hope is stored potential for a pathway not yet known.

And that gives us a lovely Pantheon instruction:

Hope is not evidence for the destination. It is permission to continue searching for the road.

That’s a real token/operator, not merely a metaphor.